;; THE ONE PLACE THE PDF CODE READS ITS INPUT (P1.4, D058).
;;
;; `emon.pdflex`, `emon.pdfdoc` and `emon.pdffilter` never call `blob-len` or
;; `blob-u8` themselves: they read the file, an object stream's decoded bytes
;; and a filter's input through the two functions below. emon's input is an
;; ABSTRACT BYTE SOURCE (no path, no file system: the library has to run in
;; WASM in a browser, where the bytes may arrive in ranges, not as one slab),
;; and the day the source becomes a window or a range reader, this is the file
;; that changes; nothing that parses a PDF does.
;;
;; TODAY A SOURCE IS A `Blob`, a whole file in memory, and both functions are
;; the port's reads. The contract a richer source must keep: `(ps-len src)` is
;; the number of bytes; `(ps-u8 src i)` is the byte at an index 0 <= i <
;; `(ps-len src)` and raises (the port's out-of-range) outside it. No parser
;; reads out of range on purpose: each bounds its reads by `ps-len` or by the
;; `end` it was given.
;;
;; THE OTHER HALF IS `cells-bytes` (emon.port): it makes a source out of bytes
;; kin built in a `Cells` (a decoded object stream, the next stage of a filter
;; chain). A windowed source would provide its own version of that.
(ns emon.pdfsrc
  (:require [emon.lang :refer [I32 Blob]]
            [emon.port :refer [blob-len blob-u8]]
            [kin.lang :refer [defn return comment]]))

(defn ^:pub ^I32 ps-len [^Blob src]
  (comment "The number of bytes in the source.")
  (return (blob-len src)))

(defn ^:pub ^I32 ps-u8 [^Blob src ^I32 i]
  (comment "The byte at index i, 0..255.")
  (return (blob-u8 src i)))
